// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row tuple_index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding (tier 1).
// `q.N = v` WHERE `q` IS A LOCAL `&mut (T, U)` WRITES INTO `q`'s OWN STORAGE.
//
// The TUPLE-INDEX twin of index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding, and
// the two are ONE root, not two: gen_expr_kind(EAddrOfTempView) routes BOTH
// `IndexRead` and `is_mut && TupleIndex` into the same call, MLIRGenImpl::gen_lvalue_addr
// (src/compiler/mlir_gen_expr.cpp), and that is where the address is wrong.
//
// ROOT, by symbol: MLIRGenImpl::gen_lvalue_addr, `case ec::Code::VarRef`
// (src/compiler/mlir_gen_expr.cpp). It consults `var_local_ptrs_` — which holds
// only RAW `*mut`/`*const` locals — and, missing there, returns
// `get_subscript_ptr(vn)` under the comment "or the pointer value (ref/ptr params)".
// That comment states the assumption that fails: for a ref/ptr PARAMETER the scope_
// entry really is the caller's address, so the GEP lands on the referent; for a
// reference-typed LOCAL the scope_ entry is an alloca HOLDING the pointer, and the
// GEP lands on that alloca. One missing load.
//
// ⚠ THE RECORDED ROOT OF THE INDEX ROW IS AT THE WRONG LAYER. That row's header
// names the `pc == la::INDEX_READ` branch of SemaChecker::lower_place_assign
// (sema_stmt.cpp) and its `addr_of_temp` call. Sema is CORRECT there: it emits
// `AddrOfTemp(IndexRead(VarRef q))`, which is the right shape. The mis-addressing
// is one layer down, in codegen.
//
// CONTROLS, measured 2026-09-06 on this binary — the class is exactly
// {IndexRead, TupleIndex} x reference-typed LOCAL:
//     (*q).0 = 77             explicit deref, tuple                  -> 77  correct
//     q.a = 77                FieldRead through a local &mut S       -> 77  correct
//     s[0] = 77               `&mut [i64]` SLICE through a local     -> 77  correct
//     fn f(q: &mut [i64;2])   index write through a PARAMETER        -> 77  correct
//     let q = &mut *s; q[0]=  index write through a REBORROW to a
//                             LOCAL, inside a fn with a &mut param   ->  1  WRONG
// The reborrow control is what rules out "parameter vs local function-argument" as
// the discriminator: what matters is that the BINDING is a local slot holding a
// pointer, however that pointer was obtained.
//
// Rust: exit 0 (t.0 becomes 77).
package tuple_index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut t: (i64, i64) = (1i64, 2i64);
    let q: &mut (i64, i64) = &mut t;
    q.0 = 77i64;
    unsafe { printf("t0=%ld\n".as_ptr(), t.0); }
    if t.0 != 77i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
